\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


For positive integers $x,y$, compare the square $y^2$ and the cube $x^3$.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Is there an absolute constant $C>0$ such that every $x,y\ge1$ with $y^2\ne x^3$ satisfies \[|y^2-x^3|\ge C\max\{y^2,x^3\}^{1/6}?\] \href{https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0312440}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
Is every nonzero gap between a positive square and a positive cube at least a fixed constant times the sixth root of their larger value?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{description}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems, record 8700015 (AMR-086-0015). CC BY 4.0. \url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] Michel Waldschmidt, Open Diophantine Problems, arXiv:math/0312440v2 (24 January 2004), Conjecture 2.7. \url{https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0312440}.
\end{description}
